WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.357
Before a client can contact a service, it often
resolves a hostname to an address. DNS

00:00:05.357 --> 00:00:11.088
distributes this discovery work across a
hierarchy and caches answers near clients,
avoiding

00:00:11.088 --> 00:00:13.704
a single central lookup for every request.

00:00:13.704 --> 00:00:18.827
An authoritative service can provide different
addresses to different resolvers or return

00:00:18.827 --> 00:00:23.778
several candidate addresses. This example
separates a client's local resolver from the

00:00:23.778 --> 00:00:29.017
authority that supplies an answer when needed.
Many clients may share one cached answer, so

00:00:29.017 --> 00:00:32.989
varying DNS responses does not imply perfectly
even per-request load.

00:00:33.204 --> 00:00:38.360
After discovery, the client connects to the
selected address; DNS is not necessarily

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:42.349
consulted again for every application request on
that connection.

00:00:42.349 --> 00:00:47.549
Caching makes a DNS change gradual rather than
instantaneous. Suppose a resolver stores

00:00:47.549 --> 00:00:52.749
Region East at time zero with a ninety-second
TTL. At time twenty, the authority begins

00:00:52.749 --> 00:00:58.068
returning Region West to new lookups. A client
asking the same resolver at time fifty can

00:00:58.068 --> 00:01:03.149
still receive East under the ordinary cache
model, because that cached answer has not

00:01:03.149 --> 00:01:03.627
expired.

00:01:03.849 --> 00:01:08.676
At time ninety, the resolver can refresh and
obtain West. This example puts the

00:01:08.676 --> 00:01:13.990
authoritative change above the still-valid
cached copy to explain the overlap. Existing

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:19.428
connections and client behavior add further
complications, so a TTL is not a promise that

00:01:19.428 --> 00:01:22.604
every user switches regions at precisely one
moment.
